ody in Florida Senate Race", "source": "Newsweek", "published": "2026-08-18T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipAFBVV95cUxNVkJQYU5PVUpoRzRKcmxudUR4MG9Mc0VxME5XNUJLS1JnQ3Z3WVpBRm5tQlBBQTVkY214WmRyU3p0M29WOHVsOGpJZlRhRFRNVEIzbEtxY0MxdUgzU3VMRUgwR1VTTVcxRUNKOFhwZ3lNWTdmcWhpZFEwNW1tckZCMXVlQlRNa3JGOEhzbEtyOElDdUNBSTRINHFVZjB0LS1MSjVxUA?oc=5"}, {"title": "Democratic socialist defeats Vindman to take on Moody for Florida Senate", "source": "The Hill", "published": "2026-08-18T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -7.53, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": -2.59, "poll_sd": 5.53, "poll_weight": 0.45, "fundamentals": {"margin": -2.31, "lean": -5.0, "environment": 7.29, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": -1.6}, "rating_margin": -11.0, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.29, "baseline_margin": -5.3, "analyst_adjustment": -0.2, "key_factors": ["Florida's Republican lean (R+5 baseline) with an incumbent appointee, Ashley Moody, on the ballot", "Moody's overwhelming financial edge: $11.3M raised and $8.5M on hand versus under $1M for Nixon", "Recent polls mixed — two show ties, but R-sponsored surveys show Moody up 7-8", "Prediction markets price Nixon's chances around 10%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a mid-single-digit Republican win: Florida's lean, Moody's incumbency and massive cash advantage, and a polling average near R+3 to R+5, with markets at roughly 90% for Moody. Some September polls show a tied race, justifying only a modest nudge toward Nixon from the ratings consensus, not a full shift.", "watch": "Whether the independent September polls showing a 45-45 race reflect real tightening or house-effect noise; a follow-up nonpartisan survey breaking Nixon's way would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Appointed Senator Ashley Moody, a well-funded Republican statewide officeholder, faces state Representative Angie Nixon, a Democratic Socialist backed by Bernie Sanders, in a special election in a state that leans about five points Republican. Moody has raised over $11 million and holds $8.5 million on hand, while Nixon has raised under $1 million with minimal outside help.\n\nPolling has narrowed in September: two surveys show the race tied at 45 or 47, though Republican-sponsored polls still show Moody ahead by seven or eight. Prediction markets give Nixon roughly a one-in-ten chance, and most race raters place this in the Likely or Solid Republican column. The race is competitive enough to watch but Moody remains clearly favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1404, "margin": -5.31}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2259, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1936, "margin": -5.5}]}